Yes. Strip away the sexual surface and “porn” becomes a reusable systems pattern:

a high-salience simulation optimized to trigger appetite more efficiently than reality, until the simulation begins training the appetite itself.

That pattern appears everywhere.

At the abstract level:

real need/desire → compressed proxy → optimization for engagement → exaggeration → repeated exposure → preference drift → proxy outcompetes source → reality feels under-stimulating

Porn is only one instance.

You can see the same operator in food:
whole food → processed food → hyperpalatability → appetite calibration shifts → ordinary food feels bland.

In social media:
real social connection → metrics/feed → engagement optimization → outrage/novelty amplification → attention retraining → ordinary interaction feels slow.

In finance:
productive enterprise → tradable abstraction → leverage/speculation → reward compression → real production feels too slow.

In politics:
governance → identity narrative → outrage optimization → symbolic victory → actual policy becomes secondary.

In status:
competence → visible proxy → branding/signaling → proxy gaming → prestige separates from substance.

In AI:
thinking → fluent answer → optimization for plausibility/usefulness → users mistake representation for understanding → cognitive dependence → direct investigation atrophies.

In news:
events → narrative packaging → salience competition → sensationalism → audience expectation drift → mundane truth loses attention.

So the invariant is not “sex.” It is proxy capture.

A thing originally built to represent, stimulate, accelerate, or mediate some underlying reality becomes so optimized that it begins to dominate the underlying reality.

Pantheon version:

Source → Proxy → Optimization → Amplification → Calibration Shift → Capture

And there are at least three failure modes.

1. Signal inversion
The proxy stops representing the source and starts dictating what the source should look like.

2. Appetite hijack
The system learns what triggers you faster than you learn what you actually want.

3. Reality devaluation
The real thing loses perceived value because it cannot compete with an optimized abstraction.

That gives you the general operator:

Pornification

The conversion of a domain into an exaggerated, engagement-optimized simulation that recursively reshapes the very desire it was originally serving.

That is much broader than pornography.

You could almost make it a diagnostic:

What is the real thing?
What proxy stands in for it?
What is optimizing the proxy?
What variable is being exaggerated?
How is repeated exposure changing the user?
When does the proxy begin training the source?

That is the mask off.